  Backport: content to be taken from branch main @ cd9d9c63254f471f966ee51b078afdf47af5f852
            (2026-06-19, release 1.3.26 for NeoForge 1.21.1) and from the branches 1.20.4 and
            1.21 in between

Modifications
-------------
  Modified by SkyTekx (the ts4.party community), 2026, for the SkyTekx 3 modpack. The result
  still runs on Minecraft 1.20.1 with Forge 47, keeps the mod id actuallyadditions and is
  versioned 1.1.52-skytekx.1.

  Summary of the changes:
    * Build: reproducible jar (no file timestamps, fixed entry order, no build timestamp in the
      manifest). This NOTICE and the upstream LICENSE ship inside the jar. mods.toml marks the
      build as a modified, unofficial one and sends bug reports to SkyTekx.
    * Build: the hash cache of the data generator (.cache/) no longer ends up in the jar. The
      build compiles against Patchouli and the Curios API and registers the mixin config
      actuallyadditions.mixins.json through the jar manifest, as Forge 1.20.1 expects.
    * Metadata: mods.toml declares Patchouli, Curios and JEI as optional dependencies, and
      pack.mcmeta uses pack_format 15, the format of Minecraft 1.20.1, instead of the 1.16 value.
    * Left out of the source: the fully commented out classes
      de.ellpeck.actuallyadditions.mod.util.crafting.RecipeHandler and RecipeHelper and
      de.ellpeck.actuallyadditions.mod.inventory.ContainerGiantChest (they compiled to nothing),
      and the two Inventory Tweaks API annotations in the package invtweaks.api.container, which
      upstream removed on its 1.20.4 line too.
    * Special renders: the client reads the list of upstream's main branch instead of the one on
      the 1.16 branch, as upstream does since its 1.21 line.
    * Config: actuallyadditions-common.toml has the keys of upstream's main branch with its
      camelCase names, and a new actuallyadditions-client.toml holds hideEnergyOverlay. Forge no
      longer rewrites the common config at every load.
    * Models and data: the Actually Additions Manual and the Storage Crate Keeper have item
      models, flat items use the generated item model and tools the handheld one, crops render
      with cutout, and crushing recipes with one output no longer list an empty second one.
    * Client: the empty ClientRegistryHandler is gone (upstream registered it on the wrong
      event bus and later deleted it).
    * Tags: black quartz, its ore and block, the crystal blocks, the four crops and their seeds,
      the rice slimeball, stairs, slabs, walls and the AIOTs carry the forge: and vanilla tags of
      upstream's newer lines, with each c: tag of upstream's main branch mapped to its Forge name.
    * Recipes: the machines, lamps and blocks that had no recipe can be crafted as on upstream's
      main branch, crystal blocks compress and decompress, black quartz ore smelts, and walls,
      slabs and stairs give the vanilla amounts and have stonecutter recipes.
    * Recipes: the Lens of the Killer accepts a real Sharpness V enchanted book. The base and
      upstream's 1.20.4 line asked for a book with the tool enchantment tag, which no book has.
      The 3x3 slime block recipe of the mod takes rice slimeballs instead of every
      forge:slimeballs item, so that it no longer clashes with the slime block recipes of other
      mods.
    * Advancements: every advancement of the Actually Additions tab can be earned, and their
      titles are in title case as on upstream's main branch.
    * JEI: a category for the Lens of the Miner, and the Atomic Reconstructor shows as a
      machine, not as an ingredient, so EMI's recipe tree no longer counts one per craft.
    * Dedicated server: block overlays, the Engineer's Goggles tick and the clientbound packet
      handlers live in client only classes, and common classes no longer name client classes
      outside code that only the physical client runs. Menus of the Item Filter, the drill and
      the Advanced Laser Relay show their titles.
    * Network: the server checks every screen packet against its sender (own level, loaded
      block within 8 blocks, own entity id) and only accepts the handlers meant for its side.
      Dropped and failing packets log a WARN that names the player, at most 20 per player in
      60 seconds. The protocol version is 5.
    * Menus: the drill, the Item Filter and the firework box close like the other screens, and
      the drill writes its upgrades only into the drill it was opened with (a client could
      duplicate upgrades by switching the hotbar slot while the drill screen was open).
    * Player data: the client receives its player data again, and the unused data of the old
      booklet (bookmarks, trials, tutorial flag) is no longer saved.
    * Firework Box: numbers outside the ranges of its sliders are clamped when they arrive and
      when the box loads, so a crafted packet or save can no longer crash its tick.
    * Assets: the textures and block models of upstream's 1.20+ retexture with four stage crops
      and fixed AIOT sprites, mono machine sounds, and none of the 1.12 leftovers (old
      blockstates, models, textures, loot tables, a structure and mcmod.info) or the unused
      creative tab background in the jar any more.
    * Block shapes: outlines and collision boxes follow the new models as on upstream's main
      branch, with simple shapes for the generators, stands and the Phantom Booster and full
      blocks for the machines whose models now fill the block.
    * Crystal clusters: they grow out of the face they are placed on, need a solid block behind
      them, and drop crystal shards unless broken with silk touch, as on upstream's main branch.
    * Lamps: unlit lamps no longer look lit, a lamp toggled by hand changes for every player,
      a dye in the off hand no longer takes the item of the main hand, and the Lamp Controller
      faces the player and switches at most 4096 connected lamps without deep recursion.
    * Sounds: casings sound like their material, machines like metal and crystal blocks and
      clusters like amethyst, as on upstream's main branch.
    * Breakers and placers: the Long-Range Breaker and the Vertical Digger mine and collect their
      drops, the Breaker breaks blocks that need a tool, such as snow, the Placer and the
      Phantom Breaker work, and the Phantom Placer has its side button. The Phantom Breaker
      keeps the contents of a bound shulker box, and neither it nor the Long-Range Breaker
      deletes water, lava or other fluid blocks. The Vertical Digger mines down to the bottom of
      the world, keeps its layer across restarts and restarts once from its own height when it
      was saved by an older build.
    * Phantomfaces: pipes connected to a face follow it when it is bound elsewhere or when the
      target is loaded again, the look at overlay shows the target, the Player Interface keeps
      its player after a restart, reaches 32 blocks and no longer throws without a player, and
      the Player Probe binds again.
    * Fluid machines: the Fluid Placer places again without wasting fluid, keeps fluids that
      have no block in its tank and drops itself when mined with a pickaxe, and the Fluid
      Collector also empties full water and lava cauldrons.
    * Farmer: a compass sets its work area, it plants nether wart again, leaves modded farmland
      alone and tills modded soil into its own farmland, and planting makes a sound.
    * Atomic Reconstructor: its beam and the other lasers render as particles, the renderer no
      longer crashes, death messages and tooltips work, lenses stack, the Lens of Color keeps
      stack sizes, and the Lens of the Miner makes copper, black quartz, ancient debris and
      deepslate ores, plus the ores of Mekanism, Immersive Engineering, Powah and ElementalCraft
      when those mods are loaded, without doubling the chances on deepslate.
    * Display Stand and Empowerer: the hand swings when an item goes in, and the empowered
      emeradic crystals ask for short grass instead of tall grass.
    * Greenhouse Glass: it is visible again, with no faces between two panes, and it reaches
      plants below Y 0.
    * Crushers: the Double Crusher works, shift click fills the crushers, their screens show
      their own background, and the crushing recipes of upstream's main branch, among them the
      copper and deepslate ores, come with a JEI category.
    * Powered Furnace: JEI lists it under smelting and opens that category from its screen, the
      auto split button no longer covers the energy bar, and the item has its tooltip again.
    * Coal Generator: it burns every furnace fuel at 20 CF/t instead of six fixed fuels, shows
      what a fuel gives in its screen and no longer darkens its screen twice. The unused solid
      fuel recipe type stays registered for data packs.
    * Generators: the Leaf-Eating Generator follows its config and eats leaves again, and the Bio
      Reactor faces the player, renders its model and shows its energy bar.
    * Coffee: the Coffee Maker brews drinkable coffee from empty cups, uses its water and
      takes cups and ingredients from automation. Its ingredients are data driven recipes with a
      JEI category as on upstream's main branch, effects stack without doubling, and a cup gives
      back an empty cup and can no longer be repaired. Milk weakens the effects before it and
      adds two minutes, as in the 1.12 versions, which upstream's main branch dropped.
    * Canola: JEI shows the pressing recipes of the Canola Press and the progress of the
      Fermenting Barrel, and shift click into the press works on a client of a server.
    * Energizer and Enervator: both have armor slots that only take fitting armor and keep cursed
      armor, an off hand slot and titles, and their input holds one item, which stops a
      duplication of stacked energy items.
    * Experience Solidifier: the screen has its buttons again, and the server refuses negative
      button ids.
    * Firework Box: its settings screen works, with sliders that send their value when they are
      let go and the background of upstream's main branch.
    * Automatic Feeder: it shows the animal count in its screen, has a title, and its comparator
      output follows the number of animals against the limit, as on upstream's main branch.

  The complete source of this build, with every change as a separate commit, is in
  https://github.com/skytekx/skytekx3-1201-server under ports/actuallyadditions.
  ports/actuallyadditions/UPSTREAM.md lists every change to the upstream build.

